The test file at `packages/profile/src/seed-qa-bank.test.ts` covers the following:
- resumes with no work-authorization statement (empty experience) → no assertive pair (lines 212, 241)
- text with claims → approved pair (lines 255, 318, 334, 535, 551, 661)
- A `claimDoubleWorker` helper (line 26) that probably injects claim text.

There is no test for denial (DENIES) at all, and no test for a claim + denial. So the bug I've identified holds:

**Inside `usWorkAuthorizationAnswers` (packages/profile/src/seed-qa-bank.ts:611-657)**: the `if (claim) return {…yes…}` branch on lines 629-643 executes before the `if (denies)` branch on lines 645-655. A profile whose text contains both a work-authorization claim and an explicit denial ("I previously held an H-1B work visa, but I am not authorized to work in the United States") ends up with "Yes, I am currently authorized to work in the United States" seeded and approved. This violates AGENTS.md's permanent floor: "never assert an authorization that the profile denies." The module's own docstring (lines 589-594) says the denial case "is precisely what must be pinned, because otherwise a future free-answering agent will invent an affirmative out of ambiguous text."

This is the same class of bug as the conservative-default one: an affirmative to a legal-status question wins over evidence of the negative, with the affirmative written to the bank as approved.

Fix: evaluate the denial before the claim. Move the `denies` check above the `claim` branch:

```ts
const claim = CLAIMS_US_AUTHORIZATION.exec(joinText);
const denies = DENIES_AUTHORIZATION.test(joinText);
if (denies) { return no-pair }
if (claim) { return yes-pair }
return null;
```

Is denial-beats-claim always correct? The denial regex is tightly scoped to work authorization: `not authorized to work`, `no work authorization`, `no valid work visa`, `do not have work authorization`. If any of those phrases appears in the profile, the profile is asserting (or the candidate is stating in a past-tense context) that they lack authorization… hmm, one caveat: past tense. "I am no longer on an H-1B, I had no work authorization at the time, but now I have a green card." → denies matches ("no work authorization" appears in "had no work authorization") and claim matches "green card" → after the fix, denial wins → seed "No, I am not currently authorized to work in the United States" — but the candidate actually has a green card! That would be a false negative seed.

Hmm. How realistic is this? It's a tradeoff between two failure modes:
- Claim beats denial: "I had an H-1B… not authorized to work" → YES seed. Violates the floor (asserts authorization the profile denies). A misrepresentation to a real employer.
- Denial beats claim: "had no work authorization, but now I have a green card" → NO seed. Costs one job, and the human can fix the row in /answers.

The doctrine is explicitly asymmetric: "never assert a credential, authorization, or any employment or factual matter that the profile denies or does not support. This is not a wall, because a truthful negative is always available." And "an affirmative without evidence is precisely the fabrication this module exists to prevent." A wrong YES is a misrepresentation to an employer; a wrong NO is a missed job, and the human reviews every seeded row (all rows show up on /answers for review, `approved` only means "a human has read this"). The doctrine clearly puts the negative in the win position.

Also the function's own docstring: "The candidate who explicitly states the opposite is… precisely what must be pinned, because otherwise a future free-answering agent will invent an affirmative out of ambiguous text." The stated intent is that the denial pins the answer. The current code only pins the denial when no claim is found, which contradicts the stated intent.

So: denial first. A one-line swap (well, swap two branches + a small comment).

Wait — let me also re-check the order inside the current code. Lines 626-657:

```ts
const claim = CLAIMS_US_AUTHORIZATION.exec(joinText);
const denies = DENIES_AUTHORIZATION.test(joinText);

if (claim) {
  return {
    "Are you legally authorized to work in the United States?": "Yes, ...",
    ...
  };
}

if (denies) {
  return {
    ...: "No, ...",
    ...: "No, I do not require visa sponsorship at this time.",
    __userNote: ...
  };
}

return null;
```

Fix: move `if (denies) {...}` before `if (claim) {...}`. And add a short comment explaining why the denial beats the claim.

Hmm, wait. Let me also reconsider whether it's better to make claim + denial → `null` (blank) rather than `no`. If the text both claims and denies, the profile is self-contradictory, and the safest seed is… the doctrine says "answer with the negative that claims nothing." The negative that claims nothing is exactly "No, I am not currently authorized to work in the United States." That claims nothing about the candidate beyond what the profile itself states (the profile states the denial). So the `no` seed is consistent. A blank would let a later tier (profile facts → conservative default) produce the negative anyway, but it's strictly worse than the evidence-backed `no`. Keep the `no` seed.

Now the tests. I need a test that fails before the fix and passes after. Inside `packages/profile/src/seed-qa-bank.test.ts`, a new `it` (or a new describe block). What does the test setup look like? I need to see how the existing tests call `seedQaBank` (or whatever the exported function is) with a synthetic resume/profile. Let me look at the test around lines 200-260 (the "resume with no work-authorization statement" test) and copy that harness.

Also I need to know what the function under test is called, and what it takes. Let me read the relevant section of the test file and the export signature around lines 500-560 of seed-qa-bank.ts.

I also need to double-check: is there any other consumer that depends on the claim-first behavior? Let me grep for usages of `usWorkAuthorizationAnswers` and `seedQaBank`.

